## EXACTLY HOW THE CODE READS A CALLER'S ROLE AND TENANT (develop 9cbb9687021a, re-verify the line numbers at your boot)
Role:
- `callerMayReadCertification(req, certification)` is declared at `certifications.ts:162`. It reads the principal from `(req as any).user` (line 163): `isPlatformRole(authUser?.role)` at `:172` (admits SYSTEM_ADMIN and SUPER_ADMIN, either casing, ASCII fold: `packages/shared/src/security/keyRevokePolicy.ts:140-145`), then `userId` at `:173`, the empty-principal guard `if (userId === '') return false` at `:174`, then `userId === certification.issuer?.id` at `:175` and `certification.holder?.id` at `:176`. Its doc comment (`:128-160`) says "`ORG_ADMIN` is deliberately NOT admitted" and names this card's route lines.
- The role claim is `req.user.role` (`JwtPayload.role: string`, `middleware/auth.ts:18`; `isAllowedByRoleOrScope` upper-cases it, `middleware/rbac.ts:56`; `requireRole` is case-EXACT, `middleware/auth.ts:142-151`, so do NOT use `requireRole` here).
- The one shared predicate for the exact role is `isPlatformOrOrgAdminRole(role)` at `keyRevokePolicy.ts:163-167` (ASCII fold, a non-string is false, admits SYSTEM_ADMIN, SUPER_ADMIN and ORG_ADMIN, does NOT admit ISSUER_ADMIN or ADMIN; KS-1536). It is exported from `@secuura/shared` (`packages/shared/src/index.ts:259`). The role's exact spelling in this repo is `ORG_ADMIN` (`documentWriteRoles.ts`, `gdpr.ts:172`, `keyRevokePolicy.ts:77`).
Tenant:
- `getReqTenantId(req)` at `certifications.ts:108-111` returns `req.tenantId` or the default tenant `a0000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000001`. `req.tenantId` is set in `index.ts` by `resolveRequestTenantId` (`utils/requestTenant.ts`: attached tenant, else the vouched `x-tenant-id` header, else the default).
- The route lookup IS the tenant wall: share calls `getCertification(id, getReqTenantId(req))` (`certifications.ts:1349`; `repositories/certificationRepo.ts:170-176`: `WHERE id = $1 AND tenant_id = $2`). The returned `Certification` carries NO tenant field, so "the certification's tenant" is the request tenant by construction. Recertify: `tenantId = getReqTenantId(req)` (`:1462`) then `getCertification(id, tenantId)` (`:1464`).
- The caller's own tenant is the JWT claim `req.user.tenantId` (`middleware/auth.ts:36`, "signed CONDITIONALLY" per its comment at `:24-35`). The gateway sets `x-tenant-id` from `decoded.tenantId` for a non-platform caller (`services/api-gateway/src/middleware/auth.ts:694-702`; an `X-Tenant-Override` is honoured only for a platform role).
- Precedent for comparing the claim to a target tenant: `decideTenantAccess(user, targetTenantId)` at `keyRevokePolicy.ts:184-` (exported, `index.ts:260`): platform role allow; no `user.tenantId` -> deny "caller has no tenant"; `user.tenantId !== target` -> deny "cross-tenant". Its comment says defaulting a tenant-less principal would hand it the default tenant. `services/originate` does not import it today (measured: 0 hits under `services/originate`), so this is a first use here.
The two route checks to change (develop 9cbb9687021a): share at `certifications.ts:1360-1362` (`if (!callerMayReadCertification(req, certification)) { return res.status(404)...`), recertify at `:1474-1476` (`if (!callerMayReadCertification(req, originalCert))`). Other callers of `callerMayReadCertification` (GET /:id `:1105`, lineage `:1636`, evidence-bundle `:1729`) must NOT change: Kam's ruling covers share and recertify only, and the KS-1695 cells R14 (`ks1695-...test.ts:228`) and R15 (`:398-405`) pin an ORG_ADMIN reader as 404.

## THE CHANGE (the minimum; do not guess past it)
Add ONE new helper beside `callerMayReadCertification` (do NOT widen that helper: three read routes call it), e.g.
`callerMayShareOrRecertify(req, certification)` = `callerMayReadCertification(req, certification) || (isPlatformOrOrgAdminRole(role) && decideTenantAccess(authUser, getReqTenantId(req)).allow)` where `role` and `authUser` are read from `(req as any).user` exactly as the neighbour does. Then each of the two route checks changes by ONE token (the called function's name): "one line per route". Imports: extend the existing `import { isPlatformRole } from '@secuura/shared'` at line 57 with the two names. Refusal bytes stay each route's own 404 (`Certification not found`, `Original certification not found`), position stays before the revoked-400 and before every email, anchor, write and charge. Comments cite the card and quote the ruling.
Rules the code gives for free, to be PINNED by cells and not re-decided: ISSUER_ADMIN, ADMIN, USER stay refused (the ruling names ORG_ADMIN only); a different-tenant caller is 404 at the lookup (`C2`, PR 2233 test `:358`), so the new check only ever sees a same-request-tenant row; revoke stays issuer-only 403; verify stays open.

## DEFAULTS I HAVE SET, FOR WEDNESDAY TO CONFIRM IN YOUR PLAN ANSWER (do not start the product line until it lands)
- D1 (tenant half). Default = the explicit claim compare above (`decideTenantAccess`), so an ORG_ADMIN with NO `tenantId` claim is refused (404) rather than falling to the default tenant: `requestTenant.ts` and `tenant-context.ts:75` both default a tenant-less request to the default tenant, and the claim is signed conditionally (`middleware/auth.ts:24-35`). The cheaper alternative is lookup-only (no claim compare), which admits a tenant-less ORG_ADMIN into the default tenant's rows. MEASURE in your plan how many live-shaped ORG_ADMIN principals carry no `tenantId` (the minting site is `services/auth/src/services/jwt.ts` near `:263`, per the comment in `middleware/auth.ts`; fixtures and seeded accounts) and say which spelling of the claim every portal login produces. Wednesday answers; if lookup-only, the cells S8 and R8 below become "admitted" and the cross-tenant cells run on the lookup alone.
- D2 (tenant versus organisation). Kam said "a company's own admin" and the card says "same-tenant". A tenant holds many organisations (`keyRevokePolicy.ts:68-75`, KS-764; gate BX1's "same tenant, another organisation" row). The default is TENANT only: an ORG_ADMIN of organisation A may share and recertify a certification issued in organisation B of the same tenant. Say so in the PR body as "what this admits" and put the ORG-bound variant (`decideTenantAccess`'s `organisationScope` arm; the Certification row exposes no organisation, so it is NOT a one-line change) to Wednesday as one sentence. This is Wednesday's to rule; the seat builds tenant-only unless told otherwise.
- D3 (spelling). `isPlatformOrOrgAdminRole` ASCII-folds, so `org_admin` is admitted and the dotless `org_admın` (U+0131) is NOT (KS-1591 lineage). Pin both.

